/* ==============================================================
   07 — The gripper
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     Meca500 only (R3 or R4)
   TEACHES   GripperOpen/Close, MoveGripper, force, speed, and
             the parallel-execution trap
   NEEDS     Robot activated + homed, MEGP 25E or 25LS gripper
             mounted and homed
   WATCH     The gripper moving WHILE the arm moves
   TIME      ~30 s
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THE COMMANDS

       GripperOpen()          go to the open position
       GripperClose()         go to the closed position
       MoveGripper(d)         go to a specific opening, in mm
       SetGripperForce(p)     grip force, % of max
       SetGripperVel(p)       finger speed, % of max (default 40)
       SetGripperRange(c, o)  redefine what open and closed mean

   MoveGripper is the one worth knowing: an electric gripper is
   not a two-state pneumatic cylinder. You can send the fingers
   to any opening in millimetres, which means one gripper handles
   a family of parts, and changeover is a number rather than a
   hardware swap.

   FORCE IS SOFTWARE

   SetGripperForce sets grip force as a percentage. Low force for
   something fragile, high force for something heavy, changed
   between two parts in the same cycle if you like. No regulator,
   no spring change, no tooling.

   When the fingers meet an object before reaching their target
   opening, they stop and hold it at the set force, and the
   gripper reports that it is holding a part. If the part is
   removed, the fingers continue to the opening that was asked
   for.

   THE TRAP: GRIPPER COMMANDS RUN IN PARALLEL

   This is the single most important thing on this page, and it
   surprises everyone.

   Gripper commands are queued like motion commands, but they do
   not BLOCK. From the manual: a gripper command starts at the
   beginning of the blending or deceleration phase of the
   preceding move, and it continues to execute in parallel with
   subsequent moves, which begin immediately.

   In plain terms: write GripperClose() followed by a move, and
   the arm starts leaving while the fingers are still closing.
   On the bench with a light part you may get away with it. With
   a heavier part, or a faster cycle, you drop it.

   That parallelism is a FEATURE — it is free cycle time when the
   gripper can close during the approach. But it has to be
   deliberate.

   To make it deliberate:

     - to have the gripper act only after the arm has stopped,
       put a Delay before it, or use SetCheckpoint and wait for
       it in the host
     - to keep the arm still until the gripper has finished, put
       a Delay after it, or have the host watch the gripper state
       with GetRtGripperState

   In a demo program, Delay is fine and readable. In production,
   the checkpoint and gripper-state route is the correct one,
   because a Delay is a guess about timing and a checkpoint is a
   fact.

   BEFORE YOU RUN THIS

   The gripper must be mounted and homed. Without one, the
   gripper commands error out — run this file only on a robot
   that has one fitted.

   /!\NOTE ON THE STANDARD RESET BELOW

   This is the one example where the reset is not free. Every
   reactivation re-homes an MEGP 25* gripper, so the fingers move
   each time you run this file. The robot itself does not move.
   Keep the fingers clear, and do not be surprised when the
   gripper cycles before the program proper starts.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* --- Standard reset: start from a known state -----------------
   Deactivating loses every NON-persistent setting — tool and
   world frames, speeds, accelerations, blending, payload, torque
   limits, posture and turn selection, move mode — so this
   program starts from firmware defaults no matter what ran
   before it. Reactivating brings the motors back.

   This does NOT move the arm: a robot that was already homed
   needs no re-homing. (With an MEGP 25* gripper fitted, the
   gripper re-homes on reactivation. The robot still does not
   move — but keep the fingers clear.)

   It does NOT reset persistent settings — joint limits, work
   zone, tool sphere, collision and calibration config all
   survive. Run common/cold_start.mxprog for those.

   This block is identical in every example. To remove it
   everywhere, see the README. */
DeactivateRobot()
ActivateRobot()
Home()
ResetError()
ResumeMotion()


SetJointVel(20)
SetCartLinVel(60)

MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)


/* --- Gripper settings -----------------------------------------
   Moderate force and a gentle speed, for a demo you can stand
   next to and for parts you do not want to mark. */
SetGripperVel(30)
SetGripperForce(40)

GripperOpen()
Delay(1)


/* --- Opening to a dimension ------------------------------------
   Not open or closed — specific widths in millimetres. This is
   the sequence that shows an electric gripper is not a cylinder.
   Watch the fingers stop at each width. */
MoveGripper(5)
Delay(1)
MoveGripper(3)
Delay(1)
MoveGripper(1)
Delay(1)
GripperOpen()
Delay(1)


/* --- A pick, written safely ------------------------------------
   Note the Delay after every gripper command. Each one is there
   to stop the arm moving while the fingers are still travelling.
   Remove them and this sequence looks fine right up until it
   drops a part. */

// Approach above the part
MovePose(190, 0, 290, 0, 90, 0)
GripperOpen()
Delay(1)                              // let the fingers finish

// Descend onto it
MoveLin(190, 0, 250, 0, 90, 0)
Delay(0.3)                            // let the arm settle

// Close on the part
GripperClose()
Delay(1)                              // let the grip complete

// Lift, carry, place
MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, -40, 0, 0, 0)
MovePose(190, 60, 290, 0, 90, 0)
MoveLin(190, 60, 250, 0, 90, 0)
Delay(0.3)

GripperOpen()
Delay(1)                              // let it release before lifting

MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, -40, 0, 0, 0)


/* --- Force, changed mid-cycle ----------------------------------
   Two grips on the same part at very different forces, one after
   the other. Nothing mechanical changed between them. This is a
   good moment to ask what changing grip force costs on a
   pneumatic tool. */

SetGripperForce(10)                   // gentle
GripperClose()
Delay(1.5)
GripperOpen()
Delay(1)

SetGripperForce(100)                  // firm
GripperClose()
Delay(1.5)
GripperOpen()
Delay(1)


// --- Leave things tidy ----------------------------------------
SetGripperForce(40)
SetGripperVel(40)
GripperOpen()
Delay(1)
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THINGS TO TRY

   1. The MoveGripper width sequence is the demo. One gripper,
      any opening, changed in software. Ask how many gripper
      variants they currently stock.

   2. The force block is the second demo. Fragile part and heavy
      part in the same cycle, no hardware change.

   3. If you want to show the parallel-execution behaviour on
      purpose, delete the Delay after a GripperClose and run it.
      The arm leaves while the fingers are still moving. Better
      that a rep sees this on a bench than a customer sees it in
      a cell.

   NEXT   advanced/01_variables.mxprog — programs that are not
          hard-coded.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
